\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Literature context of the 5$\sigma $ trigger thresholds, in total power (\S  \ref  {sec:background}). \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}EIRP against distance. Blue: this survey, deepest window per star (94{} stars, 1.30--39.63\,pc; filled: Class\nobreakspace  {}A, fine-channel; open: Class\nobreakspace  {}B, coarse-channel); orange: \citet  {Enriquez2017}; aqua: \citet  {Margot2023}; violet: \citet  {Mason2024}; left-edge ticks: deepest per-target limits of \citet  {Price2020}. Grey dotted: EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux. Green dashed: the Arecibo 2.38-GHz EIRP benchmark, a power scale only (\S  \ref  {sec:benchmarks}). \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}Native channel width against observing frequency for the same windows and programmes: resolutions differ by ${\sim }10^{7}$, so the panels must be read together and a lower EIRP here does not imply greater sensitivity to a hertz-wide carrier. \textbf  {This is not a like-for-like sensitivity comparison.} The $5\sigma $ plotted for each programme is that programme's own threshold statistic, and those statistics differ in definition, in the channel width they are evaluated over and in the completeness they correspond to; for this survey it is a trigger power with 42--61{} per cent recovery, not a detection limit (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:nomenclature}). Our plotted values also exclude the $\times 2.29$ median spectral-response correction and the $\times 1.41$ available from the individual parallel hands, so a transmitter comparison carries a further $\times 3.2$ against this survey's curve at the median response, more in the tail.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:context}{{1}{3}{Literature context of the 5$\sigma $ trigger thresholds, in total power (\S \ref {sec:background}). \emph {(a)}~EIRP against distance. Blue: this survey, deepest window per star (\NStars {} stars, \DistMin --\DistMax \,pc; filled: Class~A, fine-channel; open: Class~B, coarse-channel); orange: \citet {Enriquez2017}; aqua: \citet {Margot2023}; violet: \citet {Mason2024}; left-edge ticks: deepest per-target limits of \citet {Price2020}. Grey dotted: EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux. Green dashed: the Arecibo \ArecGHz -GHz EIRP benchmark, a power scale only (\S \ref {sec:benchmarks}). \emph {(b)}~Native channel width against observing frequency for the same windows and programmes: resolutions differ by ${\sim }10^{7}$, so the panels must be read together and a lower EIRP here does not imply greater sensitivity to a hertz-wide carrier. \textbf {This is not a like-for-like sensitivity comparison.} The $5\sigma $ plotted for each programme is that programme's own threshold statistic, and those statistics differ in definition, in the channel width they are evaluated over and in the completeness they correspond to; for this survey it is a trigger power with \RecThresh --\StratRecFive {} per cent recovery, not a detection limit (Table~\ref {tab:nomenclature}). Our plotted values also exclude the $\times \HanFacMed $ median spectral-response correction and the $\times \PolGain $ available from the individual parallel hands, so a transmitter comparison carries a further $\times \DebitCombined $ against this survey's curve at the median response, more in the tail}{figure.1}{}}
