\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The 4{} stage-1 spatial outliers with no astrophysical attribution, one row each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position and `ctrl' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics; $|\dot  \nu |_{\rm  max}$ is the widest drift the stack evaluated for that window; $P_{\rm  eff}$ is the response- and smearing-corrected threshold power. `Repeat blocks' counts other execution blocks of the same star whose tuning covers this frequency, with the statistic the deepest of them returns at the stellar position. Only CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713 has one, and it does not recur (\S  \ref  {sec:cp72}). None of the four has been tested in the visibility domain. $^{a}$\nobreakspace  {}the released crossing frequency is stored only where the pipeline recorded a peak; for these the window is given instead.}}{15}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:unattributed}{{6}{15}{The \NStageOneUnattrib {} stage-1 spatial outliers with no astrophysical attribution, one row each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position and `ctrl' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics; $|\dot \nu |_{\rm max}$ is the widest drift the stack evaluated for that window; $P_{\rm eff}$ is the response- and smearing-corrected threshold power. `Repeat blocks' counts other execution blocks of the same star whose tuning covers this frequency, with the statistic the deepest of them returns at the stellar position. Only CP$-$72~2713 has one, and it does not recur (\S \ref {sec:cp72}). None of the four has been tested in the visibility domain. $^{a}$~the released crossing frequency is stored only where the pipeline recorded a peak; for these the window is given instead}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Every stage-1 spatial outlier of the completed sweep, one row per star--band pair, generated from the released catalogue. $N_{\rm  win}$ is the number of flagged windows in that pair and the remaining columns describe its deepest window: $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:tstar}), `ctrl' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics, $\nu $ the crossing channel, and $\Delta v$ the offset of that channel from the nearest laboratory rest frequency of the masked species in the star's frame, signed so that a negative value places the observed channel below the rest frequency. A pair is dispositioned as circumstellar or foreground emission when $|\Delta v|\leq 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and independent evidence exists, and as unattributed otherwise; the $4{}$ unattributed pairs are consistent with the 3.2{} expected by chance ($p=0.40$). The HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 row remains the limiting case, a near-tie of rank dispositioned by velocity coincidence with independently published emission.}}{15}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:flagged}{{7}{15}{Every stage-1 spatial outlier of the completed sweep, one row per star--band pair, generated from the released catalogue. $N_{\rm win}$ is the number of flagged windows in that pair and the remaining columns describe its deepest window: $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.~\ref {eq:tstar}), `ctrl' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics, $\nu $ the crossing channel, and $\Delta v$ the offset of that channel from the nearest laboratory rest frequency of the masked species in the star's frame, signed so that a negative value places the observed channel below the rest frequency. A pair is dispositioned as circumstellar or foreground emission when $|\Delta v|\leq \MaskVWidth $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and independent evidence exists, and as unattributed otherwise; the $\NStageOneUnattrib {}$ unattributed pairs are consistent with the \ExpStageOneAll {} expected by chance ($p=\PStageOneUnattrib $). The HD~48370 row remains the limiting case, a near-tie of rank dispositioned by velocity coincidence with independently published emission}{table.7}{}}
