\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Why the spatial control ensemble is a calibrated screen and not an exchangeable rank test. \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}add-one rank of the star among its 512{} controls, for the survey and for the calibration windows carrying no attributed line: both lie above the uniform diagonal. The dotted curve is the \emph  {pseudo-stars}, control positions at the inner edge of the annulus ranked against the other controls, which contain no star and are displaced just as far (\S  \ref  {sec:radial}), so the displacement is instrumental. \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}the control statistic itself against radius from the pointing centre, in units of each window's own noise scale. It is lowest at mid-annulus and rises towards both edges; the shaded strip inside $0.14\,\theta _{\rm  PB}$ holds no control probe at all, and that is where the star sits (median 0.0032$\,\theta _{\rm  PB}$).}}{18}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rankcdf}{{5}{18}{Why the spatial control ensemble is a calibrated screen and not an exchangeable rank test. \emph {(a)}~add-one rank of the star among its \NCtrl {} controls, for the survey and for the calibration windows carrying no attributed line: both lie above the uniform diagonal. The dotted curve is the \emph {pseudo-stars}, control positions at the inner edge of the annulus ranked against the other controls, which contain no star and are displaced just as far (\S \ref {sec:radial}), so the displacement is instrumental. \emph {(b)}~the control statistic itself against radius from the pointing centre, in units of each window's own noise scale. It is lowest at mid-annulus and rises towards both edges; the shaded strip inside $\RadRIn \,\theta _{\rm PB}$ holds no control probe at all, and that is where the star sits (median \RadUStarMed $\,\theta _{\rm PB}$)}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713 repeat test in full, both blocks side by side, so the reader can tell ``not detected again'' from ``a persistent signal of the original strength would have been recovered''. The repeat block is the deeper of the two, matches the first in tuning, channelisation and integration, and its frequency axis registers to a fifth of a channel. A source at the first block's flux would have returned $T_\star =6.21$ there; the measured value is 4.70. Intermittent emission is not excluded by this test, and no statement here assumes otherwise.}}{18}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cprepeat}{{9}{18}{The CP$-$72~2713 repeat test in full, both blocks side by side, so the reader can tell ``not detected again'' from ``a persistent signal of the original strength would have been recovered''. The repeat block is the deeper of the two, matches the first in tuning, channelisation and integration, and its frequency axis registers to a fifth of a channel. A source at the first block's flux would have returned $T_\star =\CpRecExpT $ there; the measured value is \CpTstarTwo . Intermittent emission is not excluded by this test, and no statement here assumes otherwise}{table.9}{}}
